 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "For the following exercises, determine whether each statement is always true or sometimes true.",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 542947,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A trail is a path.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "All paths are trails, but not all trails are paths. Some trails use the same vertex more than once. A path has no repeated vertices."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "sometimes true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542948,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A trail is a walk.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "All trails are walks. Trails use connected vertices, which is what it takes to be a walk."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "always true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542949,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A walk is a path.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "All paths are walks, but not all walks are paths. Some walks use the same vertex more than once. A path has no repeated vertices."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "sometimes true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542950,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A circuit is a trail.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "By definition, a circuit is a trail that begins and ends at the same vertex."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "always true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542951,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A directed cycle is a path.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "By definition, a directed cycle is a path that begins and ends at the same vertex."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "always true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542952,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A circuit is a directed cycle.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>By definition, a directed cycle is a path that begins and ends at the same vertex. A circuit begins and ends at the same vertex.</p>\n<p>A directed cycle does not necessarily start and end at the same vertex, so not all directed cycles are circuits.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "sometimes true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542953,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A directed cycle is a circuit.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>By definition, a directed cycle is a path that begins and ends at the same vertex.</p>\n<p>A circuit is a trail that begins and ends at the same vertex. Thus, a directed cycle is a circuit.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "always true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542954,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "If a graph has an <span data-math=\"n\">n</span>-coloring, then its chromatic number is <span data-math=\"n\">n</span>.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "You could color a graph with more colors than the minimum number required. For instance, you could use a different color for every vertex on a quadrilateral cycle. This graph would have a chromatic number of 2 but use 4 colors."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "sometimes true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542955,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "If the chromatic number of a graph is <span data-math=\"n\">n</span>, then it has an <span data-math=\"n\">n</span>-coloring.\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "This is the definition of the chromatic number."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "always true"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542956,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "If a graph is planar, then it has a chromatic number of at most four.\n",
